            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA reports the rover has been studying a region called the **“Broom Point member”** on the rim of Jezero Crater, where it found evidence that a **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock** was likely built up by **repeated asteroid impacts**. NASA says these rocks are probably **more than 3.9 billion years old**, making them among the oldest terrain ever examined by a Mars rover and a valuable record of early solar-system bombardment【5:1†source】.\n\nA related update says Perseverance has provided a sweeping view of **Broom Point**, looking back up at the outer lip of Jezero Crater’s rim; NASA also published an orbital map showing the rover’s route to that ancient layered bedrock site【5:1†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the equivalent of a **full marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km**. It reached that mark on its **1,890th Martian day**, after about **five years and four months** of driving, much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance【5:0†source】. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter captured Perseverance from orbit as a tiny speck near its tracks shortly before it hit that distance milestone【5:2†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active, driving beyond Jezero Crater, investigating very ancient impact-related rocks, and setting a new mobility milestone on Mars.",
